E LayerContentSource                            | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 31-39     | Where an entry's content comes from — `56` §3's four sources. reduction level was promoted, which is what `20` §2.1's third reconstruction source reads.
F SourceReconstructible                         | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 46-49     | Whether content from one source can be rebuilt from what the document holds. content itself would be classifying content it can only see as texels.
T PaintedContent                                | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 62-67     | The one thing in a document that is stored as texels rather than as a description. change of working extent or a domain re-partition resamples. Everything else is re-resolved, and re-resolution is exact where resampling is not. allocations rather than thirty times the channel count.
T CoverageSpecification                         | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 80-87     | Where an entry applies, and how strongly. Painting coverage is a stroke, undone by `22` §4's extent-bounded inverse, and nothing about it is a separate mechanism. analytically. A coverage that could only be painted would make a tiled mask impossible.
T LayerSpecification                            | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 103-115   | One entry of a surface's content, declaring all four of `56` §2's fields and nothing implicit. between impressions within a stroke; this applies it between entries. Two documents declaring two sets of behaviours produce a surface whose result changes depending on whether content arrived as a stroke or as a layer, which the artist cannot see and cannot correct. an artist may legitimately hold a layer that writes nothing while they decide; what is forbidden is defaulting to all twenty, which silently overwrites roughness with a colour layer and is discovered at export.
F EntryWritesChannel                            | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 120-124   | Whether an entry writes one of `42`'s channels.
T SurfaceLayerSequence                          | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 141-308   | The ordered content of one surface, per channel, and the only ordering authority over it. is a sequence position. priority or an ordinal that could disagree with its position, and `12`'s outliner **presents** that order rather than holding a second one — which is why reordering in the outliner and reordering in a layer panel can never disagree. `68`'s parametric domain, and `20` projects the sequence onto whatever is resident. A sequence holding device extents would make the working extent a property of the file, and changing it an import.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::Append                  | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 153       | Appends one entry at the end of the sequence, issuing its identity. painted content whose span does not match its declared extent, and with ExtentExhausted at the entry ceiling
F SurfaceLayerSequence::Reorder                 | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 164       | Moves one entry to a declared sequence position. position so the caller's inverse is one ordinal rather than a whole ordering nothing else, which is what makes reordering affordable in `RevisionSequence`.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::DeclarePresence         | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 172       | Presents or hides one entry. presented has been told the document does not hold what they see.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::DeclareCombination      | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 178       | Amends one entry's combination.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::Withdraw                | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 188       | Removes one entry, retaining its description and never its resolved texels. from a description live in `SurfaceDepot` under `20` §4 and are reconstructible by definition, so recording them in an inverse would store a derivation inside the document to undo a change that does not need it.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::Nest                    | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 200       | Nests one sequence inside this one as a single entry. ExtentExhausted beyond `LayerNestingCeiling` coverage are each applied **once**, to the nested result. Applying the enclosing coverage per entry instead is the defect that makes a partly covered nested sequence darken at its own internal overlaps — the same defect `22` §3 fixes by accumulating a stroke once and applying it once, at a different scale.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::Resample                | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 219       | Resamples every painted entry into a re-partitioned domain, on the tick. position it occupied in the old one unwrap; **this** resamples the authored texels, because `68` is in `SlateCompute` and cannot reach the document content at all. The remapping crosses as a callable so that nothing here names `68`. for exactly that reason. Everything else is re-resolved, and re-resolution is exact where resampling is not. not a defect in it; what would be a defect is performing it without saying so.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::Resolve                 | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 228       | One entry, by identity.
F SurfaceLayerSequence::AmendPainted            | Api/SurfaceLayerSequence.h       | 241       | One entry's painted texels, for the one mechanism permitted to amend them. ContentUnsupported when its source is not PaintedImpressions describes — §3 — so this is the one route by which authored content is written at all, and every write through it is inside a transaction whose inverse is bounded by the extents it touched. resolved projection the source of truth for content the description already holds, and `20` §4 would then evict the artist's edit at the first memory pressure.
